y our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\widehat{\\,y}=3.5+5x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}=3.5.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.101)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{18,0.025}=2.101.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=2.101\\cdot \\sqrt{8\\cdot (\\frac{1}{20}+\\frac{{2}^{2}}{24})}≈2.8\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=3.5\\pm 2.8\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186192979 1074 "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\widehat{\\,y}={\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,y}={\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=3.5+5x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept ${\\beta }_{0}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}}_{\\,\\,\\text{point}\\,\\text{estimate}}\\,\\pm \\,\\overbrace{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\widehat{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]}^{\\text{margin of error}}.\n$$\n\nNote