 2.5% & ✓ \\\\ P(X \\le 1)|= 1.07% < 2.5% & ✓ \\\\ P(X \\le 2)|= 5.47%≮2.5% & \\cdot\\end{bmatrix}$\n- For the right tail, $X=10$ and $X=9$ lie in the critical region, but $X=8$ does not.\n$\\begin{bmatrix}P(X \\ge 10)|= 0.1% < 2.5% & ✓ \\\\ P(X \\ge 9)|= 1.07% < 2.5% & ✓ \\\\ P(X \\ge 8)|= 5.47%≮2.5% & \\cdot\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- The critical region for this hypothesis test is\n$X\\leq 1\\quad\\textrm{or}\\quad X\\geq 9$.\n- The critical values are $X=1$ and $X=9$." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186193896 1064 "Recall that the **critical region** contains all $x$-values that are *collectively* unlikely under the null hypothesis and therefore cause the null hypothesis to be rejected.\n\nFor two-tailed tests, the critical region typically consists of *two* disjoint sets. Consequently, there are, typically, *two* critical values.\n\nThe critical region for the hypothesis test we just carried out is shown below.\n\n![](images/72/7234fd53c36f99dae631885c2693467daae4f823d1dab2b23ead7a6dbe7a9da7.png)\n\nAs we saw, $X=2$ does not lie in the critical region. Let's consider the possible value of $X$ that *do* lie in the critical region:\n\n- For the left tail, it's easy to show that the values $X=0$ and $X=1$ lie in the critical region, but $X=2$ does not.\n$\\begin{bmatrix}P(X \\le 0)|= 0.1\\% < 2.5\\% & ✓ \\\\ P(X \\le 1)|= 1.07\\% < 2.5\\% & ✓ \\\\ P(X \\le 2)|= 5.47\\%≮2.5\\% & \\cdot\\end{bmatrix}$\n- For the right tail, $X=10$ and $X=9$ lie in the critical region, but $X=8$ does not.\n$\\begin{bmatrix}P(X \\ge 10)|= 0.1\\% < 2.5\\% & ✓ \\\\ P(X \\ge 9)|= 1.07\\% < 2.5\\% & ✓ \\\\ P(X \\ge 8)|= 5.47\\%≮2.5\\% & \\cdot\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- The critical region for this hypothesis test is\n$X\\leq 1\\quad\\textrm{or}\\quad X\\geq 9$.\n- The critical values are $X=1$ and $X=9$." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186193920 1064 "We can also borrow from other place values.\n\nFor example, let's consider the equation below.\n\n$$\