{\\gamma(x,y)}^{\\psi(x,y)} f(x,y,z) + g(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nas\n\n$$\n\\int_{a}^{b} \\int_{\\alpha(x)}^{\\beta(x)} \\int_{\\gamma(x,y)}^{\\psi(x,y)} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x\n+\n\\int_{a}^{b} \\int_{\\alpha(x)}^{\\beta(x)} \\int_{\\gamma(x,y)}^{\\psi(x,y)} g(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\n**The Multiplicative Property** \n\nSuppose that $f(x)$ is continuous on $[a,b],$ $g(y)$ is continuous on $[\\alpha,\\beta],$ and $h(z)$ is continuous on $[\\gamma,\\psi].$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{a}^{b} \\int_{\\alpha}^{\\beta} \\int_{\\gamma}^{\\psi} f(x) \\cdot g(y) \\cdot h(z) \\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x\n=\n\\left( \\int_{a}^{b} f(x) \\:\\textrm{d}x \\right) \\left( \\int_{\\alpha}^{\\beta} g(y) \\:\\textrm{d}y \\right) \\left( \\int_{\\gamma}^{\\psi} h(z) \\:\\textrm{d}z \\right).\n$$\n\nThese rules are true for any order of integration, such as $\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y,$ or $\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}z,$ etc." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186199472 1064 "Repeated integrals with multiple variables obey similar rules to those we've seen before.\n\n**The Sum Rule** \n\nSuppose that $f(x,y,z)$ and $g(x,y,z)$ are continuous functions on a domain \n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y,z) \\: | \\: a \\leq x \\leq b, \\:\\: \\alpha(x) \\leq y \\leq \\beta(x), \\:\\: \\gamma(x,y) \\leq z \\leq \\psi(x,y) \\big\\}.\n$$\n\n \nThen, we can write the integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\n\\int_{a}^{b} \\int_{\\alpha(x)}^{\\beta(x)} \\int_{\\gamma(x,y)}^{\\psi(x,y)} f(x,y,z) + g(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nas\n\n$$\n\\int_{a}^{b} \\int_{\\alpha(x)}^{\\beta(x)} \\int_{\\gamma(x,y)}^{\\psi(x,y)} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x\n+\n\\int_{a}^{b} \\int_{\\alpha(x)}^{\\beta(x)} \\int_{\\gamma(x,y)}^{\\psi(x,y)} g(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\n**The Multiplicative Property** \n\nSuppose that $f(x)$ is continuous on $[a,b],$ $g(y)$ is continuous on $[\\alpha,\\beta],$ and $h(z)$ is continuous on $[\\gamma,\\psi].$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{a}^{b} \\int_{\\alpha}^{\\beta} \\int_{\\gamma}^{\\psi} f(x) \\cdot g(y) \\cdot h(z) \\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x\n=\n\\left( \\int_{a}^{b} f(x) \\:\\textrm{d}x \\right) \\left( \\int_{\\alpha}^{\\beta} g(y) \\:\\textrm{d}y \\right) \\left( \\int_{\\gamma}^{\\psi} h(z) \\:\\textrm{d}z \\right).\n$$\n\nThese rules are true for any order of integration, such as [math]\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y,[/math] or [math]\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}z,[/math] etc." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186199482 1064 "We've seen how to calculate the triple integral of a function $f(x,y,z)$ over a rectangular domain $R,$ given by \n\n$$\nR = [a,b]\\times[c,d]\\times[e,f].\n$$\n\n \n\nIn this lesson, we'll learn how to calculate triple integrals over type I regions in three-dimensional space.\n\nBefore we begin, we need to discuss the meaning behind the triple integral of a function over a non-rectangular domain. To help us, consider the region $R$ shown below.\n\n![](images/ba/ba2c52d81578e4754732a99f459b037bb7ceb35b60e3a711ed239855d837a93f.png)\n\nLet's discuss how to define the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_R 4xyz\\,\\textrm d V\n$$\n\nWe start by enclosing the region $R$ by a rectangular solid $R'$ whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axes, as shown below.\n\n![](images/e5/e5548c26288c4b8ba6ac9e450ad0a78be55ae6a6ab6e4ef7a52a086bc997ed11.png)\n\nNow, we extend the integrand to the rectangular region $R'$ by defining a new function $f(x,y,z)$ that's equal to $0$ outside $R$ and equals $4xyz$ inside $R\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4xyz, & (x,y,z)\\in {R}^{'}\\cap R \\\\ 0, & (x,y,z)\\in {R}^{'}∖R\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlthough $f(x,y,z)$ might not be continuous at the boundary of $R,$ the triple integral of $f(x,y,z)$ over the rectangular domain $R'$ will exist. \n\nAs usual, it's the unique number $I$ that satisfies the inequality\n\\[\nL_f(P) \\leq I \\leq U_f(P)\n\\]\nfor all partitions $P$ of the rectangle $R.$ \n\nTherefore, our original triple integral is defined as \n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_{R} 4xyz \\, \\textrm{d}V = \\iiint\\limits_{R'} f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}V.\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186199482 1064 "We've seen how to calculate the triple integral of a function $f(x,y,z)$ over a rectangular domain $R,$ given by \n\n$$\nR = [a,b]\\times[c,d]\\times[e,f].\n$$\n\n \n\nIn this lesson, we'll learn how to calculate triple integrals over type I regions in three-dimensional space.\n\nBefore we begin, we need to discuss the meaning behind the triple integral of a function over a non-rectangular domain. To help us, consider the region $R$ shown below.\n\n![](images/ba/ba2c52d81578e4754732a99f459b037bb7ceb35b60e3a711ed239855d837a93f.png)\n\nLet's discuss how to define the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_R 4xyz\\,\\textrm d V\n$$\n\nWe start by enclosing the region $R$ by a rectangular solid $R'$ whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axes, as shown below.\n\n![](images/e5/e5548c26288c4b8ba6ac9e450ad0a78be55ae6a6ab6e4ef7a52a086bc997ed11.png)\n\nNow, we extend the integrand to the rectangular region $R'$ by defining a new function $f(x,y,z)$ that's equal to $0$ outside $R$ and equals $4xyz$ inside $R\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4xyz, & (x,y,z)\\in {R}^{'}\\cap R \\\\ 0, & (x,y,z)\\in {R}^{'}∖R\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlthough $f(x,y,z)$ might not be continuous at the boundary of $R,$ the triple integral of $f(x,y,z)$ over the rectangular domain $R'$ will exist. \n\nAs usual, it's the unique number [math]I[/math] that satisfies the inequality\n\\[\nL_f(P) \\leq I \\leq U_f(P)\n\\]\nfor all partitions [math]P[/math] of the rectangle [math]R.[/math] \n\nTherefore, our original triple integral is defined as \n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_{R} 4xyz \\, \\textrm{d}V = \\iiint\\limits_{R'} f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}V.\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186199483 1064 "When writing down a triple integral as a repeated integral, we can often skip the intermediate step of expressing our triple integral as a mixed integral.\n\nFor example, suppose we wish to evaluate\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iiint\\limits_R f(x,y,z)  \\: \\mathrm{d}V\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the type I region given by \n\n$$\nR = \\left\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\: {\\color{red}a} \\leq x \\leq {\\color{red}b}, \\: {\\color{blue}v_1(x)} \\leq y \\leq {\\color{blue}v_2(x)}, \\: {\\color{purple}u_1(x,y)} \\leq z \\leq {\\color{purple}u_2(x,y)} \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane is a type I plane region. Therefore, we can immediately express our triple integral as a repeated integral as follows:\n\n\\[\n\\iint\\limits_{D} \\int\\limits_{u_1(x,y)}^{u_2(x,y)} f(x,y,z) \\: \\textrm{d}z \\: \\textrm{d}A,\n\\]\n\n> where $D$ is a region in the $xy$-plane. Then, we can decompose it further by writing down the double integral as a repeated integral:\n\nHowever, in many cases, the evaluation will be easier if we can decompose the triple integral into simple integrals. \nIn our example above, we can write our triple integral as a repeated integral right away (skipping the step with the double integral):\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iiint \\limits_{R} f(x,y,z)\\: \\textrm{d}V \n= \n\\int_{\\color{red}a}^{\\color{red}b} \\int_{\\color{blue}v_1(x)}^{\\color{blue}v_2(x)} \\int_{\\color{purple}u_1(x,y)}^{\\color{purple}u_2(x,y)} f(x,y,z)\\: \\mathrm{d}z \\: \\mathrm{d}y \\: \\mathrm{d}x.\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186199483 1064 "When writing down a triple integral as a repeated integral, we can often skip the intermediate step of expressing our triple integral as a mixed integral.\n\nFor example, suppose we wish to evaluate\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iiint\\limits_R f(x,y,z)  \\: \\mathrm{d}V\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the type I region given by \n\n$$\nR = \\left\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\: {\\color{red}a} \\leq x \\leq {\\color{red}b}, \\: {\\color{blue}v_1(x)} \\leq y \\leq {\\color{blue}v_2(x)}, \\: {\\color{purple}u_1(x,y)} \\leq z \\leq {\\color{purple}u_2(x,y)} \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane is a type I plane region. Therefore, we can immediately express our triple integral as a repeated integral as follows:\n\n\\[\n\\iint\\limits_{D} \\int\\limits_{u_1(x,y)}^{u_2(x,y)} f(x,y,z) \\: \\textrm{d}z \\: \\textrm{d}A,\n\\]\n\n> where [math]D[/math] is a region in the [math]xy[/math]-plane. Then, we can decompose it further by writing down the double integral as a repeated integral:\n\nHowever, in many cases, the evaluation will be easier if we can decompose the triple integral into simple integrals. \nIn our example above, we can write our triple integral as a repeated integral right away (skipping the step with the double integral):\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iiint \\limits_{R} f(x,y,z)\\: \\textrm{d}V \n= \n\\int_{\\color{red}a}^{\\color{red}b} \\int_{\\color{blue}v_1(x)}^{\\color{blue}v_2(x)} \\int_{\\color{purple}u_1(x,y)}^{\\color{purple}u_2(x,y)} f(x,y,z)\\: \\mathrm{d}z \\: \\mathrm{d}y \\: \\mathrm{d}x.\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186199585 1064 "If our predicates depend on the variable used with a quantifier, we have the following two formulas:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}∀xP(x)∧∀xQ(x) & ≡∀x(P(x)∧Q(x)) \\\\ ∃xP(x)∨∃xQ(x) & ≡∃x(P(x)∨Q(x))\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** What about $\\forall \\lor \\forall$ and $\\exists \\land \\exists$? It turns out that, in general:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}∀xP(x)∨∀xQ(x) & ≢∀x(P(x)∨Q(x)) \\\\ ∃xP(x)∧∃xQ(x) & ≢∃x(P(x)∧Q(x))\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo see why the $\\forall \\lor \\forall$ case does not hold, consider the following predicates defined over $\\mathbb{Z}{:}$\n\n- $P(x){:}$ $x$ is odd\n- $Q(x){:}$ $x$ is even\n\nThen, we have\n\n$$\n∀xP(x)∨∀xQ(x) \\mid ≡(∀x ∈ Z, [math]x[/math] is odd)∨(∀x ∈ Z, [math]x[/math] is even); = false∨false; = false\n$$\n\nIndeed, not all integers are odd, and not all integers are even. Also, the disjunction of two false statements is false.\n\nOn the other hand,\n\n$$\n∀x(P(x)∨Q(x)) \\mid ≡∀x ∈ Z([math]x[/math] is odd∨[math]x[/math] is even); = true\n$$\n\nIndeed, any integer is either even or odd. This statement is true.\n\nTherefore, $\\forall x \\, P(x) \\lor \\forall x \\, Q(x) \\not\\equiv \\forall x \\, (P(x) \\lor Q(x))$.\n\nThe proof that $\\exists x \\, P(x) \\land \\exists x \\, Q(x) \\not\\equiv \\exists x \\, (P(x) \\land Q(x))$ is similar (in fact, we can use the same counterexample).\n\nRemember that two logical formulas are equivalent if they give the same truth values \"for any interpretation,\" meaning that the left-hand side must be true *if and only if* the right-hand side is true given any possible predicates and universal sets." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186199585 1064 "If our predicates depend on the variable used with a quantifier, we have the following two formulas:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}∀xP(x)∧∀xQ(x) & ≡∀x(P(x)∧Q(x)) \\\\ ∃xP(x)∨∃xQ(x) & ≡∃x(P(x)∨Q(x))\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** What about $\\forall \\lor \\forall$ and $\\exists \\land \\exists$? It turns out that, in general:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}∀xP(x)∨∀xQ(x) & ≢∀x(P(x)∨Q(x)) \\\\ ∃xP(x)∧∃xQ(x) & ≢∃x(P(x)∧Q(x))\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo see why the $\\forall \\lor \\forall$ case does not hold, consider the following predicates defined over $\\mathbb{Z}{:}$\n\n- $P(x){:}$ $x$ is odd\n- $Q(x){:}$ $x$ is even\n\nThen, we have\n\n$$\n∀xP(x)∨∀xQ(x) \\mid ≡(∀x ∈ Z, x is odd)∨(∀x ∈ Z, x is even); = false∨false; = false\n$$\n\nIndeed, not all integers are odd, and not all integers are even. Also, the disjunction of two false statements is false.\n\nOn the other hand,\n\n$$\n∀x(P(x)∨Q(x)) \\mid ≡∀x ∈ Z(x is odd∨x is even); = true\n$$\n\nIndeed, any integer is either even or odd. This statement is true.\n\nTherefore, $\\fora