er of years after installation when the total savings will exceed the cost of the upgrade?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186208421 1074 "We are told that the hospital currently spends $\\$9,600$ per year and expects to reduce this cost to $\\$4,800$ per year after the upgrade.\n\nSo, the annual savings is\n\n$$\n9,600 - 4,800 = 4,800 \\text{dollars}\n$$\n\nLet $t$ represent the number of years after the upgrade.\n\nThen, the total savings after $t$ years is\n\n$$\n4,800t\n$$\n\nWe are told the upgrade costs $\\$30,000$, and we want to find when the *total savings exceeds the upgrade cost*. So, we write\n\n$$\n4,800t > 30,000\n$$\n\nThis is the inequality that can be solved to find $t$. Rearranging this, we could also write\n\n$$\n30,000 < 4,800t\n$$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n9,600 - 4,800 = 4,800\n$$\n\nwe can write this inequality as\n\n$$\n30,000 < (9,600 - 4,800)t\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186208421 1074 "We are told that the hospital currently spends $\\[math]9,600$ per year and expects to reduce this cost to $\\[/math]4,800$ per year after the upgrade.\n\nSo, the annual savings is\n\n$$\n9,600 - 4,800 = 4,800 \\text{dollars}\n$$\n\nLet $t$ represent the number of years after the upgrade.\n\nThen, the total savings after $t$ years is\n\n$$\n4,800t\n$$\n\nWe are told the upgrade costs $\\[math]30,000$, and we want to find when the *total savings exceeds the upgrade cost*. So, we write\n\n$$\n4,800t > 30,000\n$$\n\nThis is the inequality that can be solved to find $t$. Rearranging this, we could also write\n\n$$\n30,000 < 4,800t\n$$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n9,600 - 4,800 = 4,800\n$$\n\nwe can write this inequality as\n\n$$\n30,000 < (9,600 - 4,800)t\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186208422 1071 "A small factory produced $\\$72$, $\\$89$, $\\$96$, $\\$81$, and $\\$74$ worth of parts over five of the last six days. Find the inequality that represents the condition that the factory’s average daily production must be 