ray}}\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186208457 1074 "To determine the desired probability, we must find the ratio of the number of items that are Paints and are priced greater than $\\[math]12$ to the total number of items available.\n\nWe are told that a total of $20$ items are available. Of this amount, ${\\color{blue}4}$ are Paints priced greater than $\\[/math]12$.\n\n| Item | Priced at no more than $\\[math]12$ | Priced greater than $\\[/math]12$ | Total |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Markers | $5$ | $3$ | $8$ |\n| Paints | $8$<e/td> | $\\color{blue}4$ | $12$ |\n| **Total** | **$13$** | **$7$** | **$20$** |\n\nTherefore, the desired probability is given by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle P ( \\text{Paints and} \\ge  \\$ 12 ) & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\color{blue}4}}{20}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1 \\cdot  4}{5 \\cdot  4}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1 \\cdot  \\cancel{4}}{5 \\cdot  \\cancel{4}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{5}} .\\end{array}}\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186208469 1071 "A school survey measured the average cost of a textbook at $20$ randomly selected bookstores in New York. The mean price was estimated to be $\\$7.75$, with a margin of error of $\\$0.30$.\n\nWhich of the following is a plausible statement about the mean price of a textbook for all bookstores in New York?\n\n1. It is either less than $\\$7.45$ or greater than $\\$8.05$.\n2. It is less than $\\$7.45$.\n3. It is between $\\$7.45$ and $\\$8.05$.\n4. It is greater than $\\$8.05$." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186208469 1071 "A school survey measured the average cost of a textbook at $20$ randomly selected bookstores in New York. The mean price was estimated to be $\\[math]7.75$, with a margin of error of $\\[/math]0.30$.\n\nWhich of the following is a plausible statement about the mean price 