t{adjacent to } {P}}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that\n\n$$\n\\cos P = \\dfrac{8}{17}\n$$\n\nSo, the ratio of the adjacent to $\\angle P$ leg and the hypotenuse must be\n$8: 17$.\n\nA sketch of $\\triangle PRQ$ is shown below (drawn not to scale), where $x$ denotes some positive constant.\n\n![](images/d8/d825a08a9d6defcbf24feb6dbf4d4fce53a01548d431d0370d91040ccf292e3a.png)\n\nAccording to the Pythagorean theorem, we have that\n\n$$\nPR^2 + RQ^2 = PQ^2\n$$\n\nSolving this for $RQ$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nRQ &= \\sqrt{PQ^{2} - PR^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(17x)^{2} - (8x)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{289x^{2} - 64x^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{225x^{2}} \\\\\n&= 15x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add this to our sketch.\n\n![](images/1f/1fad5080c548bd399b9088cc66f6da72ed251ad1e9050b0b4aae50a50b98a89b.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos Q &= \\frac{adjacent to Q}{hypotenuse} \\\\\n&= \\frac{15x}{17x} \\\\\n&= \\frac{15}{17}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186212774 1074 "Recall that the cosine of an acute angle in a right triangle is given by the ratio of the adjacent leg to the hypotenuse.\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\cos P = \\dfrac{ {\\text{adjacent to } {P}}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that\n\n$$\n\\cos P = \\dfrac{8}{17}\n$$\n\nSo, the ratio of the adjacent to $\\angle P$ leg and the hypotenuse must be\n$8: 17$.\n\nA sketch of $\\triangle PRQ$ is shown below (drawn not to scale), where $x$ denotes some positive constant.\n\n![](images/d8/d825a08a9d6defcbf24feb6dbf4d4fce53a01548d431d0370d91040ccf292e3a.png)\n\nAccording to the Pythagorean theorem, we have that\n\n$$\nPR^2 + RQ^2 = PQ^2\n$$\n\nSolving this for $RQ$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nRQ &= \\sqrt{PQ^{2} - PR^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(17x)^{2} - (8x)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{289x^{2} - 64x^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{225x^{2}} \\\\\n&= 15x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add this to our sketch.\n\n![](images/1f/1fad5080c548bd399b9088cc66f6da72ed251ad1e9050b0b4aae50a50b98a89b.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos Q &= \\frac{adjacent to [math]Q[/math]}{hypotenuse} \\\\\n&= \\frac{15x}{17x} \\\\\n&= \\frac{15}{17}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186212775 1074 "Recall that the tangent of an acute angle in a right triangle is given by the ratio of the opposite leg to the adjacent leg.\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\tan A = \\dfrac{ {\\text{opposite to } {A}}}{ {\\text{adjacent to } {A}}}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that\n\n$$\n\\tan A = \\sqrt{7}\n$$\n\nSo, the ratio of the opposite to $\\angle A$ leg and the adjacent to $\\angle A$ leg must be\n$\\sqrt{7}: 1$.\n\nA sketch of $\\triangle ABC$ is shown below (drawn not to scale), where $x$ denotes some positive constant.\n\n![](images/16/1665f38e5bae34fd6b23ef48e2b23c4caa613919fe045ac2f0d01f2b98f4735a.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\tan C &= \\frac{opposite to C}{adjacent to C} \\\\\n&= \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{7}x} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186212775 1074 "Recall that the tangent of an acute angle in a right triangle is given by the ratio of the opposite leg to the adjacent leg.\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\tan A = \\dfrac{ {\\text{opposite to } {A}}}{ {\\text{adjacent to } {A}}}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that\n\n$$\n\\tan A = \\sqrt{7}\n$$\n\nSo, the ratio of the opposite to $\\angle A$ leg and the adjacent to $\\angle A$ leg must be\n$\\sqrt{7}: 1$.\n\nA sketch of $\\triangle ABC$ is shown below (drawn not to scale), where $x$ denotes some positive constant.\n\n![](images/16/1665f38e5bae34fd6b23ef48e2b23c4caa613919fe045ac2f0d01f2b98f4735a.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\tan C &= \\frac{opposite to [math]C[/math]}{adjacent to [math]C[/math]} \\\\\n&= \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{7}x} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186212784 1071 "A clothing store offers a $15\\%$ discount on all jackets. The price of a jacket varies between $\\$80$ and $\\$140$. If $S$ represents the saving that a customer can receive on a single jacket purchase, find an inequality that must be satisfied by $S$." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186212784 1071 "A clothing store offers a $15\\%$ discount on all jackets. The price of a jacket varies between $\\[math]80$ and $\\[/math]140$. If $S$ represents the saving that a customer can receive on a single jacket purchase, find an inequality that must be satisfied by $S$." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186212784 1074 "The price of the jackets varies between $\\$80$ and $\\$140$.\n\nIf the $15\\%$ discount is applied, then:\n\n- The minimum saving a customer can receive is given by\n$15\\% \\cdot \\$80 = \\$12$.\n- The maximum saving a customer can receive is given by\n$15\\% \\cdot \\$140 = \\$21$.\n\nTherefore, if $S$ represents the total saving a customer can receive, we have\n\n$$\n12 \\leq S \\leq 21\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186212784 1074 "The price of the jackets varies between $\\[math]80$ and $\\[/math]140$.\n\nIf the $15\\%$ discount is applied, then:\n\n- The minimum saving a customer can receive is given by\n$15\\% \\cdot \\[math]80 = \\[/math]12$.\n- The maximum saving a customer can receive is given by\n$15\\% \\cdot \\[math]140 = \\[/math]21$.\n\nTherefore, if $S$ represents the total saving a customer can receive, we have\n\n$$\n12 \\leq S \\leq 21\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186212789 1071 "A theme park charges $\\$65$ per person for the first $30$ people in a group and $\\$50$ for each additional person. Which function $C$ gives the total cost, in dollars, for a group of $g$ people, where $g \\geq 30$?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186212789 1071 "A theme park charges $\\[math]65$ per person for the first $30$ people in a group and $\\[/math]50$ for each additional person. Which function $C$ gives the total cost, in dollars, for a group of $g$ people, where $g \\geq 30$?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186212800 1071 "Pam and Barbara both drive to work every day. Over the past week, Pam spent the following amount of money each day on gas:\n$\\$13, \\quad \\$15, \\quad \\$18, \\quad \\$20, \\quad \\$20, \\quad \\$21$\n\nIf Barbara spent a median of $\\$25$ per day on gas, which of the following statement are true?\n\n1. Pam's median daily fuel expense is $\\$19$.\n2. Barbara's median daily fuel expense is greater than Pam's.\n3. A comparison of the medians suggests that Pam spends more money on gas per day than Barbara." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186212800 1071 "Pam and Barbara both drive to work every day. Over the past week, Pam spent the following amount of money each day on gas:\n$\\[math]13, \\quad \\[/math]15, \\quad \\[math]18, \\quad \\[/math]20, \\quad \\[math]20, \\quad \\[/math]21$\n\nIf Barbara spent a median of $\\[math]25$ per day on gas, which of the following statement are true?\n\n1. Pam's median daily fuel expense is $\\[/math]19$.\n2. Barbara's median daily fuel expense is greater than Pam's.\n3. A comparison of the medians suggests that Pam spends more money on gas per day than Barbara." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186212800 1074 "Let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that Pam's data set is already arranged from smallest to greatest. Since the data set has an even number of data points ($6$), the median is the mean of the two middle numbers:\n$13, \\: 15, \\: {\\color{blue}18}, \\: {\\color{blue}20}, \\: 20, \\:21$\nThe median of the data set is\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{{\\color{blue}18}+{\\color{blue}20}}{2}=\\dfrac{38}{2}=19.\n$$\nTherefore, Pam's median daily fuel expense is $\\$19$.\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, Barbara and Pam's median daily fuel expenses are $\\$25$ and $\\$19$, respectively.\n- Statement III is false. Since Pam's median is smaller, there is no suggestion that Pam spends more on gas per day than Barbara.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186212800 1074 "Let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that Pam's data set is already arranged from smallest to greatest. Since the data set has an even number of data points ($6$), the median is the mean of the two middle numbers:\n$13, \\: 15, \\: {\\color{blue}18}, \\: {\\color{blue}20}, \\: 20, \\:21$\nThe median of the data set is\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{{\\color{blue}18}+{\\color{blue}20}}{2}=\\dfrac{38}{2}=19.\n$$\nTherefore, Pam's median daily fuel expense is $\\[math]19$.\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, Barbara and Pam's median daily fuel expenses are $\\[/math]25$ and $\\[math]19$, respectively.\n- Statement III is false. Since Pam's median is smaller, there is no suggestion that Pam spends more on gas per day than Barbara.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186212877 1071 "| Online Order Value $(\\$)$ | Number of Orders |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $0-25$ | $8$ |\n| $25-50$ | $12$ |\n| $50-75$ | $15$ |\n| $75-100$ | $9$ |\n| $100-150$ | $6$ |\n\nThe table above shows the distribution of online order values. If an order between $\\$25$ and $\\$100$ is selected at random, what is the probability that the order is between $\\$50$ and $\\$75$?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186212877 1071 "| Online Order Value $(\\[math])$ | Number of Orders |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $0-25$ | $8$ |\n| $25-50$ | $12$ |\n| $50-75$ | $15$ |\n| $75-100$ | $9$ |\n| $100-150$ | $6$ |\n\nThe table above shows the distribution of online order values. If an order between $\\[/math]25$ and $\\[math]100$ is selected at random, what is the probability that the order is between $\\[/math]50$ and $\\[math]75$?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186212877 1074 "To determine the desired probability, we must find the fraction of orders between $\\$50$ and $\\$75$ among those between $\\$25$ and $\\$100$:\n\n$$\nP(50 \\le Value \\le 75 \\mid 25 \\mid \\le Value \\le 100); = \\frac{# orders between \\$25 and \\$100 and between \\$50 and \\$75}{# orders between \\$25 and \\$100}; = \\frac{# orders between \\$50 and \\$75}{# orders between \\$25 and \\$100}\n$$\n\nThe total number of orders between $\\$25$ and $\\$100$ is the sum of the orders in the $\\$25 - 50$, $\\$50 - 75$, and $\\$75 - 100$ groups:\n\n$$\n12 + 15 + 9 = 36\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the number of orders between $\\$50$ and $\\$75$ is the number of orders in the $\\$50 - 75$ group:\n$15$\n\nTherefore, the fraction of orders between $\\$50$ and $\\$75$ out of those between $\\$25$ and $\\$100$ is\n\n$$\nP(50 \\leq \\text{Value} \\leq 75 \\: \\mid \\: 25 \\leq \\text{Value} \\leq 100) = \\dfrac{15}{36} = \\dfrac{5}{12}\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186212877 1074 "To determine the desired probability, we must find the fraction of orders between $\\[math]50$ and $\\[/math]75$ among those between $\\[math]25$ and $\\[/math]100$:\n\n$$\nP(50 \\le Value \\le 75 \\mid 25 \\mid \\le Value \\le 100); = \\frac{# orders between \\[math]25 and \\[/math]100 and between \\[math]50 and \\[/math]75}{# orders between \\[math]25 and \\[/math]100}; = \\frac{# orders between \\[math]50 and \\[/math]75}{# orders between \\[math]25 and \\[/math]100}\n$$\n\nThe total number of orders between $\\[math]25$ and $\\[/math]100$ is the sum of the orders in the $\\[math]25 - 50$, $\\[/math]50 - 75$, and $\\[math]75 - 100$ groups:\n\n$$\n12 + 15 + 9 = 36\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the number of orders between $\\[/math]50$ and $\\[math]75$ is the number of orders in the $\\[/math]50 - 75$ group:\n$15$\n\nTherefore, the fraction of orders between $\\[math]50$ and $\\[/math]75$ out of those between $\\[math]25$ and $\\[/math]100$ is\n\n$$\nP(50 \\leq \\text{Value} \\leq 75 \\: \\mid \\: 25 \\leq \\text{Value} \\leq 100) = \\dfrac{15}{36} = \\dfrac{5}{12}\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186212929 1071 "A tutoring company charges a one-time registration fee of $\\$42$ and a monthly tuition fee of $\\$15$. If there are no additional charges, after how many months will a student have paid a total of $\\$175$?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186212929 1071 "A tutoring company charges a one-time registration fee of $\\[math]42$ and a monthly tuition fee of $\\[/math]15$. If there are no additional charges, after how many months will a student have paid a total of $\\[math]175$?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186212929 1074 "Let the number of months be $m$. The total cost includes a one-time fee of $\\$42$ and a monthly cost of $\\$15$ for each of the $m$ months, so we write this as the following equation:\n\n$$\n175 = 15m + 42\n$$\n\nTo solve for $m$, we first subtract $42$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n175 - 42 &= 15m \\\\\n133 &= 15m\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we divide both sides by $15{:}$\n\n$$\nm = \\dfrac{133}{15} = 8.86\\ldots\n$$\n\nSince the cost after exactly $8$ months would not yet reach $\\$175$, we round up to the next whole number.\n\nSo it will take *$9$ months* to reach or exceed $\\$175$ in charges." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186212929 1074 "Let the number of months be $m$. The total cost includes a one-time fee of $\\[math]42$ and a monthly cost of $\\[/math]15$ for each of the $m$ months, so we write this as the following equation:\n\n$$\n175 = 15m + 42\n$$\n\nTo solve for $m$, we first subtract $42$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n175 - 42 &= 15m \\\\\n133 &= 15m\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we divide both sides by $15{:}$\n\n$$\nm = \\dfrac{133}{15} = 8.86\\ldots\n$$\n\nSince the cost after exactly $8$ months would not yet reach $\\[math]175$, we round up to the next whole number.\n\nSo it will take *$9$ months* to reach or exceed $\\[/math]175$ in charges." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186212931 1071 "Jared bought a flash drive for $\\$6.75$ and several styluses that cost $\\$3.50$ each. If he spent a total of $\\$17.25$, which equation can be used to find $s$, the number of styluses he purchased?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186212931 1071 "Jared bought a flash drive for $\\[math]6.75$ and several styluses that cost $\\[/math]3.50$ each. If he spent a total of $\\[math]17.25$, which equation can be used to find $s$, the number of styluses he purchased?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186212931 1074 "Jared’s total cost includes $\\$6.75$ for the flash drive and $s$ styluses at $\\$3.50$ each. So the total spent on styluses is $3.50s$, and the total purchase cost was $\\$17.25$.\n\nTherefore, the equation is\n\n$$\n3.50s + 6.75 = 17.25\n$$\n\nThis equation models the total cost in terms of the number of styluses purchased." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186212931 1074 "Jared’s total cost includes $\\[math]6.75$ for the flash drive and $s$ styluses at $\\[/math]3.50$ each. So the total spent on styluses is $3.50s$, and the total purchase cost was $\\[math]17.25$.\n\nTherefore, the equation is\n\n$$\n3.50s + 6.75 = 17.25\n$$\n\nThis equation models the total cost in terms of the number of styluses purchased." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186212933 1071 "Tara has $\\$66.00$ at a gaming arcade. She buys $6$ accessories that each cost $\\$3.00$. She then uses the rest of her money to buy video game tokens that cost $\\$3.00$ each. How many tokens can Tara buy?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186212933 1071 "Tara has $\\[math]66.00$ at a gaming arcade. She buys $6$ accessories that each cost $\\[/math]3.00$. She then uses the rest of her money to buy video game tokens that cost $\\[math]3.00$ each. How many tokens can Tara buy?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186212936 1071 "A designer toy company spends $\\$38$ to manufacture a collectible figure. A reseller earns a commission of $22\\%$ of the sale price of each figure. The company’s profit on each figure is the sale price minus both the manufacturing cost and the commission. Given that the sale price is $\\$75$, find the equation representing the number of figures, $n$, the company must sell to earn a profit of $\\$10{,}494$." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186212936 1071 "A designer toy company spends $\\[math]38$ to manufacture a collectible figure. A reseller earns a commission of $22\\%$ of the sale price of each figure. The company’s profit on each figure is the sale price minus both the manufacturing cost and the commission. Given that the sale price is $\\[/math]75$, find the equation representing the number of figures, $n$, the company must sell to earn a profit of $\\[math]10{,}494$." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186212936 1074 "Let $n$ be the number of figures sold.\n\nWe are told the production cost per unit is $\\$38$, and the reseller earns $22\\%$ of the sales price. The sales price is $\\$75$, so the commission per unit is\n$0.22 \\times 75$.\n\nTo find the profit per unit, we subtract both the manufacturing cost and the commission from the sales price:\n$75 - 38 - (0.22 \\times 75)$\n\nThis simplifies to\n$(75 - 38 - 0.22 \\times 75)$.\n\nTo find the total profit, we multiply the profit per unit (given above) by the number of units $n{:}$\n$(75 - 38 - 0.22 \\times 75)n$\n\nSince the company wishes to earn a profit of $\\$10{,}494$, this gives the equation\n\n$$\n(75 - 38 - 0.22 \\times 75)n = 10{,}494\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct equation is *$(75 - 38 - 0.22 \\times 75)n = 10{,}494$*." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186212936 1074 "Let $n$ be the number of figures sold.\n\nWe are told the production cost per unit is $\\[math]38$, and the reseller earns $22\\%$ of the sales price. The sales price is $\\[/math]75$, so the commission per unit is\n$0.22 \\times 75$.\n\nTo find the profit per unit, we subtract both the manufacturing cost and the commission from the sales price:\n$75 - 38 - (0.22 \\times 75)$\n\nThis simplifies to\n$(75 - 38 - 0.22 \\times 75)$.\n\nTo find the total profit, we multiply the profit per unit (given above) by the number of units $n{:}$\n$(75 - 38 - 0.22 \\times 75)n$\n\nSince the company wishes to earn a profit of $\\[math]10{,}494$, this gives the equation\n\n$$\n(75 - 38 - 0.22 \\times 75)n = 10{,}494\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct equation is *$(75 - 38 - 0.22 \\times 75)n = 10{,}494$*." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186212946 1071 "The equation $2.85m + 4.15n = 44.85$ represents the number of muffins, $m$, and sandwiches, $n$, a café sells to total exactly $\\$44.85$. The price of each muffin is the same and the price of each sandwich is the same. What is the price of one muffin?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186212946 1071 "The equation $2.85m + 4.15n = 44.85$ represents the number of muffins, $m$, and sandwiches, $n$, a café sells to total exactly $\\[math]44.85$. The price of each muffin is the same and the price of each sandwich is the same. What is the price of one muffin?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186212946 1074 "The equation\n\n$$\n2.85m + 4.15n = 44.85\n$$\n\nmodels the total cost of buying muffins and sandwiches.\n\nThe term $2.85m$ shows the total cost of the muffins, so the coefficient $2.85$ represents the price, in dollars, of one muffin.\n\nTherefore, one muffin costs $\\$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{2.85}$." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186212946 1074 "The equation\n\n$$\n2.85m + 4.15n = 44.85\n$$\n\nmodels the total cost of buying muffins and sandwiches.\n\nThe term $2.85m$ shows the total cost of the muffins, so the coefficient $2.85$ represents the price, in dollars, of one muffin.\n\nTherefore, one muffin costs $\\[math]\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{2.85}$." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186212978 1071 "A farmer bought some hens and goats. The hens cost $\\$3.75$ each, and the goats cost $\\$4.80$ each. If 