r a school workshop. The teacher needs to purchase at least $28$ items in total, but wants to spend no more than $\\$360$. Each canvas costs $\\$12$ and each paintbrush set costs $\\$6$.\n\nWrite a system of inequalities that models this situation, and check whether the pair $(t,u)=(10,20)$ is valid for your system or not." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186216764 1071 "An art teacher is buying $t$ canvases and $u$ paintbrush sets for a school workshop. The teacher needs to purchase at least $28$ items in total, but wants to spend no more than $\\[math]360$. Each canvas costs $\\[/math]12$ and each paintbrush set costs $\\[math]6$.\n\nWrite a system of inequalities that models this situation, and check whether the pair $(t,u)=(10,20)$ is valid for your system or not." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186216764 1074 "First, we translate each part of the problem into an inequality:\n\n- There must be at least $28$ items in total, so we have\n$t + u \\geq 28$.\n- We're told that the total cost cannot exceed $\\$360$. Since each canvas costs $\\$12$ and each paintbrush set costs $\\$6$, we have\n$12t + 6u \\leq 360$.\n\nTherefore, putting it all together, the system of inequalities that models this situation is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}\\bbox[4pt,border:1px solid gainsboro]{t + u \\ge  28} \\\\ \\bbox[4pt,border:1px solid gainsboro]{12 t + 6 u \\le  360}\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nTo check if the pair\n\n$$\n(t,u)=(10,20)\n$$\n\nis a solution of the system, we substitute it into the inequalities:\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}10 + 20 \\ge  28 \\\\ 12 ( 10 ) + 6 ( 20 ) \\le  360\\end{array} \\right. }\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}30 \\ge  28 & {\\color{green}✓} \\\\ 120 + 120 = 240 \\le  360 & {\\color{green}✓}\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nSince all inequalities are satisfied, the pair $\\bbox[4pt, border: 1px solid gainsboro]{\\text{is}}$ a solution to the system.\n\nThus, purchasing 