in units, when the price is $\\[math]180$ per unit?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186216782 1074 "The linear function that models the demand $Q$, in units, for a selling price $p$, in dollars, is\n\n$$\nQ(p) = mp + b\n$$\n\nWe have two known data points:\n\n$$\n(p_{1}, Q_{1}) = (150, 24, 000); (p_{2}, Q_{2}) = (200, 18, 000)\n$$\n\nWe first find the slope $m{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\frac{Q_{2} - Q_{1}}{p_{2} - p_{1}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{18, 000 - 24, 000}{200 - 150} \\\\\n&= \\frac{-6, 000}{50} \\\\\n&=-120\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the demand function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ(p) &= mp + b \\\\\n&=-120p + b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use one of the given points, say $(150, 24,000)$, to find the value of $b{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n24, 000 &= -120 \\cdot 150 + b \\\\\n24, 000 &= -18, 000 + b \\\\\nb &= 42, 000\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\nQ(p) = -120p + 42,000\n$$\n\nTo find the demand for the price $\\$180$ per unit, we need to substitute into the above expression $p = 180$. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ(180) &= -120 \\cdot 180 + 42, 000 \\\\\n&=-21, 600 + 42, 000 \\\\\n&= 20, 400\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186216782 1074 "The linear function that models the demand $Q$, in units, for a selling price $p$, in dollars, is\n\n$$\nQ(p) = mp + b\n$$\n\nWe have two known data points:\n\n$$\n(p_{1}, Q_{1}) = (150, 24, 000); (p_{2}, Q_{2}) = (200, 18, 000)\n$$\n\nWe first find the slope $m{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\frac{Q_{2} - Q_{1}}{p_{2} - p_{1}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{18, 000 - 24, 000}{200 - 150} \\\\\n&= \\frac{-6, 000}{50} \\\\\n&=-120\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the demand function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ(p) &= mp + b \\\\\n&=-120p + b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use one of the given points, say $(150, 24,000)$, to find the value of $b{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n24, 000 &= -120 \\cdot 150 + b \\\\\n24, 000 &= -18, 000 + b \\\\\nb &= 42, 000\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo 