re. In order to earn a profit of $\\$700$, how many T-shirts must be sold?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186216784 1071 "The function $p(y) = 12y - 500$ gives the profit, in dollars, from selling $y$ personalized T-shirts on an online store. In order to earn a profit of $\\[math]700$, how many T-shirts must be sold?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186216784 1074 "We want to find $y$ such that\n\n$$\np(y) = 12y - 500 = 700\n$$\n\nSolving for $y$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12y - 500 &= 700 \\\\\n12y &= 700 + 500 \\\\\n12y &= 1, 200 \\\\\ny &= \\frac{1, 200}{12} \\\\\ny &= 100\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to earn a profit of $\\$700$, we must sell $100$ T-shirts." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186216784 1074 "We want to find $y$ such that\n\n$$\np(y) = 12y - 500 = 700\n$$\n\nSolving for $y$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12y - 500 &= 700 \\\\\n12y &= 700 + 500 \\\\\n12y &= 1, 200 \\\\\ny &= \\frac{1, 200}{12} \\\\\ny &= 100\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to earn a profit of $\\[math]700$, we must sell $100$ T-shirts." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186216786 1071 "To service an air conditioning unit, a technician charges $\\$60$ per hour for labor plus a fixed cost of $\\$150$ for parts. Write a function $C(t)$ that represents the total amount, in dollars, the technician will charge for this job if it takes $t$ hours." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186216786 1071 "To service an air conditioning unit, a technician charges $\\[math]60$ per hour for labor plus a fixed cost of $\\[/math]150$ for parts. Write a function $C(t)$ that represents the total amount, in dollars, the technician will charge for this job if it takes $t$ hours." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186216786 1074 "The total cost is made up of two parts.\n\n- The technician charges $\\$60$ for each hour of work. So for $t$ hours, the labor cost is\n\n$$\n60 \\times t\n$$\n\n- In addition, there is a fixed cost of $\\$150$ for parts.\n\nTherefore, the function that models the total cost $C$, in dollars, after $t$ hours is\n\n$$\nC(t) = 60t + 150\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186216786 1074 "The total cost is made up of two parts.\n\n- The technician charges $\\[math]60$ for each hour of work. So for $t$ hours, the labor cost is\n\n$$\n60 \\times t\n$$\n\n- In addition, there is a fixed cost of $\\[/math]150$ for parts.\n\nTherefore, the function that models the total cost $C$, in dollars, after $t$ hours is\n\n$$\nC(t) = 60t + 150\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186216788 1071 "A linear function $h(x)$ gives the total cost, in dollars, of producing $x$ custom bicycles. The total cost is $\\$720$ to produce $6$ bicycles, and $\\$1,200$ to produce $12$ bicycles. What is the equation that defines $h(x)$?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186216788 1071 "A linear function $h(x)$ gives the total cost, in dollars, of producing $x$ custom bicycles. The total cost is $\\[math]720$ to produce $6$ bicycles, and $\\[/math]1,200$ to produce $12$ bicycles. What is the equation that defines $h(x)$?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186216790 1071 "A company's total cost $h(x)$, in dollars, to produce $x$ t-shirts is given by the function\n\n$h(x) = kx + 500$,\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant and $x > 0$. The total cost to produce $40$ t-shirts is $\\$900$. What is the total cost, in dollars, to produce $300$ t-shirts?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186216790 1071 "A company's total cost $h(x)$, in dollars, to produce $x$ t-shirts is given by the function\n\n$h(x) = kx + 500$,\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant and $x > 0$. The total cost to produce $40$ t-shirts is $\\[math]900$. What is the total cost, in dollars, to produce $300$ t-shirts?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186216790 1074 "We first use the information that $h(40) = 900$ to solve for $k$. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n900 &= k \\cdot 40 + 500 \\\\\n400 &= 40k \\\\\nk &= 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the total cost function now becomes\n\n$$\nh(x) = 10x + 500\n$$\n\nThe total cost to produce $300$ t-shirts, in dollars, is $h(300)$. Therefore, we can find it by substituting $x = 300$ into the total cost function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nh(300) &= 10 \\cdot 300 + 500 \\\\\n&= 3,000 + 500 \\\\\n&= \\$3,500\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186216790 1074 "We first use the information that $h(40) = 900$ to solve for $k$. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n900 &= k \\cdot 40 + 500 \\\\\n400 &= 40k \\\\\nk &= 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the total cost function now becomes\n\n$$\nh(x) = 10x + 500\n$$\n\nThe total cost to produce $300$ t-shirts, in dollars, is $h(300)$. Therefore, we can find it by substituting $x = 300$ into the total cost function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nh(300) &= 10 \\cdot 300 + 500 \\\\\n&= 3,000 + 500 \\\\\n&= \\[math]3,500\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186216793 1071 "| Foam type | Weight per volume $\\left(\\mathrm{lb} / \\mathrm{ft}^{3}\\right)$ | Cost per pound |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Type A | $5$ | $\\$0.50$ |\n| Type B | $6$ | $\\$0.60$ |\n| Type C | $7$ | $\\$0.70$ |\n| Type D | $8$ | $\\$0.80$ |\n\nA company is creating cushions using different foam types. The table shows the weights and costs of the options. The equation\n$0.50(5a) + 0.70(7c) + 220 = 1,060$\ngives the total material cost in dollars, where $a$ is the number of $\\mathrm{ft}^3$ of Type A foam and $c$ is the number of $\\mathrm{ft}^3$ of Type C foam.\n\nWhat does the number $220$ in the equation represent?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186216793 1071 "| Foam type | Weight per volume $\\left(\\mathrm{lb} / \\mathrm{ft}^{3}\\right)$ | Cost per pound |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Type A | $5$ | $\\[math]0.50$ |\n| Type B | $6$ | $\\[/math]0.60$ |\n| Type C | $7$ | $\\[math]0.70$ |\n| Type D | $8$ | $\\[/math]0.80$ |\n\nA company is creating cushions using different foam types. The table shows the weights and costs of the options. The equation\n$0.50(5a) + 0.70(7c) + 220 = 1,060$\ngives the total material cost in dollars, where $a$ is the number of $\\mathrm{ft}^3$ of Type A foam and $c$ is the number of $\\mathrm{ft}^3$ of Type C foam.\n\nWhat does the number $220$ in the equation represent?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186216793 1074 "The equation\n\n$$\n0.50(5a) + 0.70(7c) + 220 = 1,060\n$$\n\nshows the total cost of the foam materials.\n\nThe terms $0.50(5a)$ and $0.70(7c)$ represent the costs for Type A and Type C foam.\n\nThe total cost of all foam is $\\$1,060$. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Cost for Type A} + \\text{Cost for Type C} + 220 = 1,060\n$$\n\nThat leaves $\\$220$ unaccounted for in the expression. Since the other foam types listed are Type B and Type D, this leftover cost must come from them.\n\nTherefore, the $\\$220$ represents the combined cost of the Type B and Type D foam used in the project." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186216793 1074 "The equation\n\n$$\n0.50(5a) + 0.70(7c) + 220 = 1,060\n$$\n\nshows the total cost of the foam materials.\n\nThe terms $0.50(5a)$ and $0.70(7c)$ represent the costs for Type A and Type C foam.\n\nThe total cost of all foam is $\\[math]1,060$. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Cost for Type A} + \\text{Cost for Type C} + 220 = 1,060\n$$\n\nThat leaves $\\[/math]220$ unaccounted for in the expression. Since the other foam types listed are Type B and Type D, this leftover cost must come from them.\n\nTherefore, the $\\[math]220$ represents the combined cost of the Type B and Type D foam used in the project." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186216965 1071 "A pair of gloves has a regular price of $\\$35$. During a weekend sale, the gloves are sold at a price that is $40\\%$ less than the regular price. This sale price is also $40\\%$ more than what the retailer paid per pair. What was the retailer’s cost for each pair of gloves?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186216965 1071 "A pair of gloves has a regular price of $\\[math]35$. During a weekend sale, the gloves are sold at a price that is $40\\%$ less than the regular price. This sale price is also $40\\%$ more than what the retailer paid per pair. What was the retailer’s cost for each pair of gloves?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186216965 1074 "There is a $40\\%$ discount on the regular price. Here, the regular price is $\\$35$. So, to calculate the amount of the discount, we need to find $40\\%$ of $\\$35$.\n\nTo do this, we multiply $35$ by $40\\%{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 40 \\mathrm{\\% } \\times  35 & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{40}{100}} \\times  35 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 0.40 \\times  35 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 14\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nSo, the amount of the discount is $\\$14$. We subtract this amount from the regular price:\n\n$$\n35 - 14 = 21\n$$\n\nHence, the sale price of a pair of gloves is $\\$21$.\n\nNow, we're also told that the sale price is $40\\%$ more than the retailer’s cost per pair. So, the sale price is\n\n$$\n100\\% + 40\\% = 140\\%\n$$\n\nof the retailer’s cost.\n\nWe found that the sale price is $\\$21$. So, if $p$ was the retailer’s cost for each pair, then we have the following equation, which we can solve for $p{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 140 \\mathrm{\\% } \\times  p & \\displaystyle  = 21 \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{140}{100}} \\times  p & \\displaystyle  = 21 \\\\ \\displaystyle 1.4 p & \\displaystyle  = 21 \\\\ \\displaystyle p & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{21}{1.4}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 15\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the retailer’s cost per pair of gloves was $\\$15$." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186216965 1074 "There is a $40\\%$ discount on the regular price. Here, the regular price is $\\[math]35$. So, to calculate the amount of the discount, we need to find $40\\%$ of $\\[/math]35$.\n\nTo do this, we multiply $35$ by $40\\%{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 40 \\mathrm{\\% } \\times  35 & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{40}{100}} \\times  35 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 0.40 \\times  35 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 14\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nSo, the amount of the discount is $\\[math]14$. We subtract this amount from the regular price:\n\n$$\n35 - 14 = 21\n$$\n\nHence, the sale price of a pair of gloves is $\\[/math]21$.\n\nNow, we're also told that the sale price is $40\\%$ more than the retailer’s cost per pair. So, the sale price is\n\n$$\n100\\% + 40\\% = 140\\%\n$$\n\nof the retailer’s cost.\n\nWe found that the sale price is $\\[math]21$. So, if $p$ was the retailer’s cost for each pair, then we have the following equation, which we can solve for $p{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 140 \\mathrm{\\% } \\times  p & \\displaystyle  = 21 \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{140}{100}} \\times  p & \\displaystyle  = 21 \\\\ \\displaystyle 1.4 p & \\displaystyle  = 21 \\\\ \\displaystyle p & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{21}{1.4}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 15\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the retailer’s cost per pair of gloves was $\\[/math]15$." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186216967 1071 "A bike shop buys helmets at a wholesale cost of $\\$20.00$ each and sells them at a retail price that is $180\\%$ of the wholesale cost. During clearance, each remaining helmet is sold at a $40\\%$ discount from the retail price. What is the discounted price of a helmet?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186216967 1071 "A bike shop buys helmets at a wholesale cost of $\\[math]20.00$ each and sells them at a retail price that is $180\\%$ of the wholesale cost. During clearance, each remaining helmet is sold at a $40\\%$ discount from the retail price. What is the discounted price of a helmet?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186216967 1074 "First, we are told that the retail price is $180\\%$ of the wholesale cost. Here, the wholesale cost is $\\$20.00$. So, to calculate the retail price, we multiply $20$ by $180\\%{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 180 \\mathrm{\\% } \\times  20 & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{180}{100}} \\times  20 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 1.8 \\times  20 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 36\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nHence, the retail price of a helmet is $\\$36.00$.\n\nNow, we're also told that there is a $40\\%$ discount on the retail price. So, to calculate the amount of the discount, we need to find $40\\%$ of $\\$36.00$.\n\nTo do this, we multiply $36$ by $40\\%{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 40 \\mathrm{\\% } \\times  36 & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{40}{100}} \\times  36 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 0.40 \\times  36 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 14.4\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nSo, the amount of the discount is $\\$14.40$. We subtract this amount from the retail price:\n\n$$\n36 - 14.4 = 21.6\n$$\n\nTherefore, the discounted price of a helmet is $\\$21.60$." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186216967 1074 "First, we are told that the retail price is $180\\%$ of the wholesale cost. Here, the wholesale cost is $\\[math]20.00$. So, to calculate the retail price, we multiply $20$ by $180\\%{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 180 \\mathrm{\\% } \\times  20 & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{180}{100}} \\times  20 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 1.8 \\times  20 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 36\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nHence, the retail price of a helmet is $\\[/math]36.00$.\n\nNow, we're also told that there is a $40\\%$ discount on the retail price. So, to calculate the amount of the discount, we need to find $40\\%$ of $\\[math]36.00$.\n\nTo do this, we multiply $36$ by $40\\%{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 40 \\mathrm{\\% } \\times  36 & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{40}{100}} \\times  36 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 0.40 \\times  36 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 14.4\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nSo, the amount of the discount is $\\[/math]14.40$. We subtract this amount from the retail price:\n\n$$\n36 - 14.4 = 21.6\n$$\n\nTherefore, the discounted price of a helmet is $\\[math]21.60$." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186216968 1071 "A luggage store lists a travel backpack with a set regular price. During a holiday promotion, the backpack is sold at a price that is $20\\%$ less than the regular price. The discounted price is $140\\%$ of the final selling price. If the final selling price is $\\$32.00$, what is the regular price?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186216968 1071 "A luggage store lists a travel backpack with a set regular price. During a holiday promotion, the backpack is sold at a price that is $20\\%$ less than the regular price. The discounted price is $140\\%$ of the final selling price. If the final selling price is $\\[math]32.00$, what is the regular price?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186216968 1074 "Let $p$ be the regular price.\n\nThere is a $20\\%$ discount on the regular price. Subtract this from the regular price:\n\n$$\np - 0.20p = 0.80p\n$$\n\nHence, the discounted price is $0.80p$ dollars.\n\nWe are also told that the discounted price is $140\\%$ of the final selling price. Here, the final selling price is $\\$32.00$. So,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 140 \\mathrm{\\% } \\times  32 & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{140}{100}} \\times  32 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 1.4 \\times  32 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 44.8 .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nThus, the discounted price is $\\$44.80$. Since the discounted price also equals $0.80p$, we solve for $p{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.80p &= 44.8 \\\\\np &= \\frac{44.8}{0.80} \\\\\n&= 56\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the regular price is $\\$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{56.00}$." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186216968 1074 "Let $p$ be the regular price.\n\nThere is a $20\\%$ discount on the regular price. Subtract this from the regular price:\n\n$$\np - 0.20p = 0.80p\n$$\n\nHence, the discounted price is $0.80p$ dollars.\n\nWe are also told that the discounted price is $140\\%$ of the final selling price. Here, the final selling price is $\\[math]32.00$. So,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 140 \\mathrm{\\% } \\times  32 & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{140}{100}} \\times  32 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 1.4 \\times  32 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 44.8 .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nThus, the discounted price is $\\[/math]44.80$. Since the discounted price also equals $0.80p$, we solve for $p{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.80p &= 44.8 \\\\\np &= \\frac{44.8}{0.80} \\\\\n&= 56\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the regular price is $\\[math]\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{56.00}$." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186216969 1071 "A craft store sells cutting mats at a retail price that is $180\\%$ of the wholesale cost. During a seasonal sale, each mat is discounted by $25\\%$ from the retail price. If the final selling price of a mat is $\\$33.75$, what is the wholesale cost per mat?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186216969 1071 "A craft store sells cutting mats at a retail price that is $180\\%$ of the wholesale cost. During a seasonal sale, each mat is discounted by $25\\%$ from the retail price. If the final selling price of a mat is $\\[math]33.75$, what is the wholesale cost per mat?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186216969 1074 "Let $p$ be the wholesale cost per mat.\n\nFirst, we are told that the retail price is $180\\%$ of the wholesale cost. So, to calculate the retail price, we multiply $p$ by $180\\%{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 180 \\m