ities, represent them symbolically, and interpret their solutions. Let's start with an example.\n\nSuppose a bakery manager is buying $x$ trays and $y$ boxes for a catering order. The manager needs to purchase at least $36$ items in total, but wants to spend no more than $\\$180$. Each tray costs $\\$8$ and each box costs $\\$2$.\n\nTo model this situation, we translate each condition into an inequality:\n\n- The total number of items is equal to $x+y$. There must be at least $36$ items in total, so we have\n$x + y \\geq 36$.\n- Since each tray costs $\\$8$ and each box costs $\\$2$, their total cost is $8x + 2y$. We're told that it cannot exceed $\\$180$. So we have\n$8x + 2y \\leq 180$.\n\nTherefore, putting it all together, the system of inequalities that models this situation is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x + y \\ge  36 \\\\ 8 x + 2 y \\le  180\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nThis system models all possible choices of trays and boxes that both satisfy the minimum item requirement and stay within the budget.\n\n**Watch out!** We consider only non-negative pairs, as the number of items can't be negative." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186218217 1064 "In this lesson, we'll learn how to translate constraints into systems of linear inequalities, represent them symbolically, and interpret their solutions. Let's start with an example.\n\nSuppose a bakery manager is buying $x$ trays and $y$ boxes for a catering order. The manager needs to purchase at least $36$ items in total, but wants to spend no more than $\\[math]180$. Each tray costs $\\[/math]8$ and each box costs $\\[math]2$.\n\nTo model this situation, we translate each condition into an inequality:\n\n- The total number of items is equal to $x+y$. There must be at least $36$ items in total, so we have\n$x + y \\geq 36$.\n- Since each tray costs $\\[/math]8$ and each box costs $\\[math]2$, their total cost is $8x + 2y$. We're told that it cannot exceed $\\[/math]180$. So we have\n$8x + 2y \\leq 180$.\n\nTherefore, putting it all together, the system of inequalities that models this situation is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x + y \\ge  36 \\\\ 8 x + 2 y \\le  180\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nThis system models all possible choices of trays and boxes that both satisfy the minimum item requirement and stay within the budget.\n\n**Watch out!** We consider only non-negative pairs, as the number of items can't be negative." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186218219 1064 "In the bakery example from the beginning of the lesson, we wrote the system\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x + y \\ge  36 \\\\ 8 x + 2 y \\le  180\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nbecause the problem said that the manager wanted\n\n- to buy *at least $36$ items*, and\n- to spend *no more than $\\$180$.*\n\nBoth these phrases lead to *non-strict inequalities* (\n\n$$\n\\geq\n$$\n\n,\n\n$$\n\\leq\n$$\n\n).\n\nBut if the manager instead wants *more than $36$ items*, then the first inequality in the system becomes strict, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x + y \\hspace{0.222em} {\\color{blue}>} \\hspace{0.222em} 36 \\\\ 8 x + 2 y \\le  180\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nThe most common phrases and the corresponding inequality symbols are listed in the table below.\n\n| **Phrasing** | **Symbol** |\n| --- | ---: |\n| at least, no less than | $\\geq$ |\n| at most, no more than | $\\leq$ |\n| more than, greater than | $\\gt$ |\n| less than, fewer than | $\\lt$ |" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186218219 1064 "In the bakery example from the beginning of the lesson, we wrote the system\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x + y \\ge  36 \\\\ 8 x + 2 y \\le  180\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nbecause the problem said that the manager wanted\n\n- to buy *at least $36$ items*, and\n- to spend *no more than $\\[math]180$.*\n\nBoth these phrases lead to *non-strict inequalities* (\n\n$$\n\\geq\n$$\n\n,\n\n$$\n\\leq\n$$\n\n).\n\nBut if the manager instead wants *more than $36$ items*, then the first inequality in the system becomes strict, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x + y \\hspace{0.222em} {\\color{blue}>} \\hspace{0.222em} 36 \\\\ 8 x + 2 y \\le  180\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nThe most common phrases and the corresponding inequality symbols are listed in the table below.\n\n| **Phrasing** | **Symbol** |\n| --- | ---: |\n| at least, no less than | $\\geq$ |\n| at most, no more than | $\\leq$ |\n| more than, greater than | $\\gt$ |\n| less than, fewer than | $\\lt$ |" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186218220 1064 "Sometimes, a situation has *more than two conditions* that must be satisfied. Let’s return to the bakery manager example from the beginning of the lesson. The manager wanted\n\n- to buy at least $36$ items, and\n- to spend no more than $\\$180$.\n\nRecall that the system of inequalities that models this situation looked as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x + y \\ge  36 \\\\ 8 x + 2 y \\le  180\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nIn addition to these conditions, suppose the manager also decides to buy:\n\n- more than $5$ trays, which can be written as\n${\\color{blue}x > 5}$.\n- no more than $50$ boxes, which can be written as\n${\\color{red}y \\leq 50}$.\n\nTherefore, the full system is now the following:\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x + y \\ge  36 \\\\ 8 x + 2 y \\le  180 \\\\ {\\color{blue}x > 5} \\\\ {\\color{red}y \\le  50}\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nLet's see some more examples." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186218220 1064 "Sometimes, a situation has *more than two conditions* that must be satisfied. Let’s return to the bakery manager example from the beginning of the lesson. The manager wanted\n\n- to buy at least $36$ items, and\n- to spend no more than $\\[math]180$.\n\nRecall that the system of inequalities that models this situation looked as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x + y \\ge  36 \\\\ 8 x + 2 y \\le  180\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nIn addition to these conditions, suppose the manager also decides to buy:\n\n- more than $5$ trays, which can be written as\n${\\color{blue}x > 5}$.\n- no more than $50$ boxes, which can be written as\n${\\color{red}y \\leq 50}$.\n\nTherefore, the full system is now the following:\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x + y \\ge  36 \\\\ 8 x + 2 y \\le  180 \\\\ {\\color{blue}x > 5} \\\\ {\\color{red}y \\le  50}\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nLet's see some more examples." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186218322 1064 "When a price changes over several steps—like a discount followed by a cost calculation—we can compute each stage one at a time. We then solve it step by step. First, we find the discounted or marked-up price. Then, we apply the next percentage (such as cost or tax) to that result.\n\nSolving multi-part percentage problems in steps makes them much easier to handle.\n\nTo illustrate, consider the following example:\n\n> A movie ticket has a regular price of $\\$45.00$. During a special promotion, the ticket is sold at a discounted price that is $80\\%$ of the regular price. The theater’s cost per ticket is $25\\%$ of the discounted price. What is the theater’s cost for each ticket?\n\nFirst, let's find the discounted price. The discounted price is given as $80\\%$ of the regular price. Here, the regular price is $\\$45.00$. So, to calculate the discounted price, we multiply $45$ by $80\\%{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 80 \\mathrm{\\% } \\times  45 & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{80}{100}} \\times  45 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 0.8 \\times  45 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 36\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nHence, the discounted price of a ticket is $\\$36.00$. Now, we can find the theater's cost per ticket.\n\nWe're also told that the theater’s cost per ticket is $25\\%$ of the discounted price. So, to calculate the theater’s cost per ticket, we multiply $36$ by $25\\%{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 25 \\mathrm{\\% } \\times  36 & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{25}{100}} \\times  36 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 0.25 \\times  36 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 9\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the theater’s cost per ticket is $\\$9.00$.\n\nThe following diagram provides a schematic overview of the steps involved in solving this problem.\n\n![](images/49/49eeb5e3a63e788cf9924f431aada229d1f43c7021d7d50471be21f27af20245.png)\n\nLet's see another example." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186218322 1064 "When a price changes over several steps—like a discount followed by a cost calculation—we can compute each stage one at a time. We then solve it step by step. First, we find the discounted or marked-up price. Then, we apply the next percentage (such as cost or tax) to that result.\n\nSolving multi-part percentage problems in steps makes them much easier to handle.\n\nTo illustrate, consider the following example:\n\n> A movie ticket has a regular price of $\\[math]45.00$. During a special promotion, the ticket is sold at a discounted price that is $80\\%$ of the regular price. The theater’s cost per ticket is $25\\%$ of the discounted price. What is the theater’s cost for each ticket?\n\nFirst, let's find the discounted price. The discounted price is given as $80\\%$ of the regular price. Here, the regular price is $\\[/math]45.00$. So, to calculate the discounted price, we multiply $45$ by $80\\%{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 80 \\mathrm{\\% } \\times  45 & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{80}{100}} \\times  45 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 0.8 \\times  45 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 36\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nHence, the discounted price of a ticket is $\\[math]36.00$. Now, we can find the theater's cost per ticket.\n\nWe're also told that the theater’s cost per ticket is $25\\%$ of the discounted price. So, to calculate the theater’s cost per ticket, we multiply $36$ by $25\\%{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 25 \\mathrm{\\% } \\times  36 & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{25}{100}} \\times  36 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 0.25 \\times  36 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 9\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the theater’s cost per ticket is $\\[/math]9.00$.\n\nThe following diagram provides a schematic overview of the steps involved in solving this problem.\n\n![](images/49/49eeb5e3a63e788cf9924f431aada229d1f43c7021d7d50471be21f27af20245.png)\n\nLet's see another example." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186218347 1064 "\\[\ny=x y'+g(y')\n\\]\nwhere $g$ is a continuously differentiable function.\n\nA **Clairaut differential equation** is an ordinary differential equation of the form\n\n$$\ny=x y'+g(y')\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a continuously differentiable function.\n\nFor example, consider the equation\n\n$$\ny = xy' + (y')^{2}.\n$$\n\nThis is a Clairaut differential equation with\n\n$$\ng(y') = (y')^{2}.\n$$\n\nClairaut equations have a special structure that allows them to be solved using elementary methods, leading to a family of straight-line solutions and a singular solution.\n\nLater in the lesson, we will learn how to solve these equations. For now, let’s practice identifying them." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186218347 1064 "\\[\ny=x y'+g(y')\n\\]\nwhere [math]g[/math] is a continuously differentiable function.\n\nA **Clairaut differential equation** is an ordinary differential equation of the form\n\n$$\ny=x y'+g(y')\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a continuously differentiable function.\n\nFor example, consider the equation\n\n$$\ny = xy' + (y')^{2}.\n$$\n\nThis is a Clairaut differential equation with\n\n$$\ng(y') = (y')^{2}.\n$$\n\nClairaut equations have a special structure that allows them to be solved using elementary methods, leading to a family of straight-line solutions and a singular solution.\n\nLater in the lesson, we will learn how to solve these equations. For now, let’s practice identifying them." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186220987 1071 "Jamal works part-time building websites. He earns $\\$20$ per hour for the first $4$ hours each week and $\\$26$ per hour for any additional hours. He saves $65\\%$ of his weekly earnings. What is the minimum number of additional hours he must work to save at least $\\$325$?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186220987 1071 "Jamal works part-time building websites. He earns $\\[math]20$ per hour for the first $4$ hours each week and $\\[/math]26$ per hour for any additional hours. He saves $65\\%$ of his weekly earnings. What is the minimum number of additional hours he must work to save at least $\\[math]325$?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186220987 1074 "Let $x$ be the number of additional hours Jamal works beyond the first $4$ hours.\n\nHe earns $\\$20$ per hour for the first $4$ hours:\n\n$$\n20 \\cdot 4 = 80\n$$\n\nHe earns $\\$26$ for each additional hour:\n$26x$\n\nHis total earnings are\n$80 + 26x$.\n\nHe saves $65\\%$ of his earnings:\n$0.65(80 + 26x)$.\n\nHe wants to save *at least* $\\$325$. We write this as the following inequality:\n\n$$\n0.65(80 + 26x) \\geq 325\n$$\n\nThen, we divide both sides by $0.65$:\n\n$$\n80 + 26x \\geq \\dfrac{325}{0.65} = 500\n$$\n\nNow, we subtract $80$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n26x \\geq 420\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides by $26$:\n\n$$\nx \\geq \\dfrac{420}{26} \\approx 16.15\n$$\n\nSince he cannot work a fraction of an hour, he must work *at least $17$ additional hours*." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186220987 1074 "Let $x$ be the number of additional hours Jamal works beyond the first $4$ hours.\n\nHe earns $\\[math]20$ per hour for the first $4$ hours:\n\n$$\n20 \\cdot 4 = 80\n$$\n\nHe earns $\\[/math]26$ for each additional hour:\n$26x$\n\nHis total earnings are\n$80 + 26x$.\n\nHe saves $65\\%$ of his earnings:\n$0.65(80 + 26x)$.\n\nHe wants to save *at least* $\\[math]325$. We write this as the following inequality:\n\n$$\n0.65(80 + 26x) \\geq 325\n$$\n\nThen, we divide both sides by $0.65$:\n\n$$\n80 + 26x \\geq \\dfrac{325}{0.65} = 500\n$$\n\nNow, we subtract $80$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n26x \\geq 420\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides by $26$:\n\n$$\nx \\geq \\dfrac{420}{26} \\approx 16.15\n$$\n\nSince he cannot work a fraction of an hour, he must work *at least $17$ additional hours*." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186220988 1071 "A robotics club is buying identical toolkits for each of the $50$ members. The total budget for the purchase is $\\$7,000$, which includes a $6\\%$ sales tax. What is the maximum possible price per toolkit, before sales tax, that the club can afford within the budget, rounded to two decimal places?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186220988 1071 "A robotics club is buying identical toolkits for each of the $50$ members. The total budget for the purchase is $\\[math]7,000$, which includes a $6\\%$ sales tax. What is the maximum possible price per toolkit, before sales tax, that the club can afford within the budget, rounded to two decimal places?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186220988 1074 "Let $x$ represent the pre-tax price of one toolkit. The cost for $50$ toolkits before tax is $50x$. With $6\\%$ sales tax, the total cost becomes\n\n$$\n50x \\cdot 1.06\n$$\n\nThis amount must be less than or equal to the budget:\n\n$$\n50x \\cdot 1.06 \\leq 7,000\n$$\n\nNow, we divide both sides by $1.06{:}$\n\n$$\n50x \\leq \\dfrac{7,000}{1.06} \\approx 6,603.77\n$$\n\nFinally, dividing both sides by $50$ yields:\n\n$$\nx \\leq \\dfrac{6,603.77}{50} \\approx 132.08\n$$\n\nSo, the maximum possible price per toolkit, before sales tax, is approximately $\\$132.08$." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186220988 1074 "Let $x$ represent the pre-tax price of one toolkit. The cost for $50$ toolkits before tax is $50x$. With $6\\%$ sales tax, the total cost becomes\n\n$$\n50x \\cdot 1.06\n$$\n\nThis amount must be less than or equal to the budget:\n\n$$\n50x \\cdot 1.06 \\leq 7,000\n$$\n\nNow, we divide both sides by $1.06{:}$\n\n$$\n50x \\leq \\dfrac{7,000}{1.06} \\approx 6,603.77\n$$\n\nFinally, dividing both sides by $50$ yields:\n\n$$\nx \\leq \\dfrac{6,603.77}{50} \\approx 132.08\n$$\n\nSo, the maximum possible price per toolkit, before sales tax, is approximately $\\[math]132.08$." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186220990 1071 "A dance studio offers a monthly unlimited class pass for $\\$135$. Individual classes cost $\\$7.00$, $\\$8.75$, or $\\$9.50$, depending on the class type. What is the minimum number of classes someone must attend for the unlimited pass to be more cost-effective than paying per class?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186220990 1071 "A dance studio offers a monthly unlimited class pass for $\\[math]135$. Individual classes cost $\\[/math]7.00$, $\\[math]8.75$, or $\\[/math]9.50$, depending on the class type. What is the minimum number of classes someone must attend for the unlimited pass to be more cost-effective than paying per class?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186220990 1074 "To find the fewest number of classes that makes the unlimited pass worthwhile, we assume each class costs the highest price of $\\$9.50$.\n\nLet $x$ represent the number of classes attended.\n\nWithout the pass, the total cost is $\\$9.50$ per class:\n$9.50x$\n\nWith the pass, the cost is a flat $\\$135$.\n\nWe want the pass to cost less than paying individually. This gives the inequality:\n\n$$\n135 < 9.50x\n$$\n\nNow, solve the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}135 & < 9.50x \\\\ \\frac{135}{9.50} & < x \\\\ 14.210… & < x\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSince $x$ must be a whole number, the smallest value that satisfies the inequality is $15$." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186220990 1074 "To find the fewest number of classes that makes the unlimited pass worthwhile, we assume each class costs the highest price of $\\[math]9.50$.\n\nLet $x$ represent the number of classes attended.\n\nWithout the pass, the total cost is $\\[/math]9.50$ per class:\n$9.50x$\n\nWith the pass, the cost is a flat $\\[math]135$.\n\nWe want the pass to cost less than paying individually. This gives the inequality:\n\n$$\n135 < 9.50x\n$$\n\nNow, solve the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}135 & < 9.50x \\\\ \\frac{135}{9.50} & < x \\\\ 14.210… & < x\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSince $x$ must be a whole number, the smallest value that satisfies the inequality is $15$." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186220991 1071 "A tutor is planning a weekend workshop. The venue rental is a one-time cost of $\\$35$, and each participant receives materials costing $\\$18.25$. If the tutor’s total budget is $\\$200$, what is the greatest number of participants that can attend without exceeding the budget?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186220991 1071 "A tutor is planning a weekend workshop. The venue rental is a one-time cost of $\\[math]35$, and each participant receives materials costing $\\[/math]18.25$. If the tutor’s total budget is $\\[math]200$, what is the greatest number of participants that can attend without exceeding the budget?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186220991 1074 "We are given a scenario with a fixed cost and a per-participant cost. We are told the total cost must not exceed $\\$200$, and we are asked to find the maximum number of participants the tutor can afford.\n\nLet $x$ be the number of participants.\n\nThe total cost is the fixed fee plus the per-participant fee:\n\n$$\n\\text{Total cost} = 35 + 18.25x\n$$\n\nWe are told this total must not exceed $\\$200$, so we write the inequality:\n\n$$\n35 + 18.25x \\leq 200\n$$\n\nWe now solve the inequality for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}35 + 18.25x & \\le 200 \\\\ 18.25x & \\le 200 - 35 \\\\ 18.25x & \\le 165 \\\\ x & \\le \\frac{165}{18.25} \\\\ x & \\le 9.0410…\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSince $x$ must be a whole number, the greatest possible number of participants is $9$." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186220991 1074 "We are given a scenario with a fixed cost and a per-participant cost. We are told the total cost must not exceed $\\[math]200$, and we are asked to find the maximum number of participants the tutor can afford.\n\nLet $x$ be the number of participants.\n\nThe total cost is the fixed fee plus the per-participant fee:\n\n$$\n\\text{Total cost} = 35 + 18.25x\n$$\n\nWe are told this total must not exceed $\\[/math]200$, so we write the inequality:\n\n$$\n35 + 18.25x \\leq 200\n$$\n\nWe now solve the inequality for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}35 + 18.25x & \\le 200 \\\\ 18.25x & \\le 200 - 35 \\\\ 18.25x & \\le 165 \\\\ x & \\le \\frac{165}{18.25} \\\\ x & \\le 9.0410…\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSince $x$ must be a whole number, the greatest possible number of participants is $9$." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186220992 1071 "Noah is buying supply kits for a robotics challenge. There is a one-time handling fee of $\\$17$, and each kit costs $\\$16$. Noah must spend *at least* $\\$113$ in total. If he can only purchase a whole number of kits, what is the minimum number of kits he must order?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186220992 1071 "Noah is buying supply kits for a robotics challenge. There is a one-time handling fee of $\\[math]17$, and each kit costs $\\[/math]16$. Noah must spend *at least* $\\[math]113$ in total. If he can only purchase a whole number of kits, what is the minimum number of kits he must order?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186220998 1071 "A factory worker is assembling products. Each widget of type $\\textrm{X}$ requires $\\$15$ in parts, and each widget of type $\\textrm{Y}$ requires $\\$12$. The total value of parts used must be no less than $\\$360$. If $x$ represents the number of type $\\textrm{X}$ widgets and $y$ represents the number of type $\\textrm{Y}$ widgets, find the inequality that represents this situation." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186220998 1071 "A factory worker is assembling products. Each widget of type $\\textrm{X}$ requires $\\[math]15$ in parts, and each widget of type $\\textrm{Y}$ requires $\\[/math]12$. The total value of parts used must be no less than $\\[math]360$. If $x$ represents the number of type $\\textrm{X}$ widgets and $y$ represents the number of type $\\textrm{Y}$ widgets, find the inequality that represents this situation." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186220998 1074 "The worker is building $x$ type $\\textrm{X}$ widgets at $\\$15$ each and $y$ type $\\textrm{Y}$ widgets at $\\$12$ each.\n\nSo, the total cost is\n$15x + 12y$.\n\nWe are told the value must be *no less than* $\\$360$, and so we have\n\n$$\n15x + 12y \\geq 360\n$$\n\nThus, the correct inequality is\n\n$$\n15x + 12y \\geq 360\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186220998 1074 "The worker is building $x$ type $\\textrm{X}$ widgets at $\\[math]15$ each and $y$ type $\\textrm{Y}$ widgets at $\\[/math]12$ each.\n\nSo, the total cost is\n$15x + 12y$.\n\nWe are told the value must be *no less than* $\\[math]360$, and so we have\n\n$$\n15x + 12y \\geq 360\n$$\n\nThus, the correct inequality is\n\n$$\n15x + 12y \\geq 360\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186221002 1071 "A farmer is purchasing $x$ bags of grain and $y$ water tanks for livestock. The farmer must purchase at least $25$ items in total, but wants to spend no more than $\\$7,000$. Each bag of grain costs $\\$120$ and each water tank costs $\\$350$, and the farmer must buy at least $10$ bags of grain and at least $5$ water tanks. What is the system of inequalities representing this situation?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186221002 1071 "A farmer is purchasing $x$ bags of grain and $y$ water tanks for livestock. The farmer must purchase at least $25$ items in total, but wants to spend no more than $\\[math]7,000$. Each bag of grain costs $\\[/math]120$ and each water tank costs $\\[math]350$, and the farmer must buy at least $10$ bags of grain and at least $5$ water tanks. What is the system of inequalities representing this situation?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186221002 1074 "First, we translate each condition into an inequality:\n\n- We're told that the total cost cannot exceed $\\$7,000$. Since each bag of grain costs $\\$120$ and each water tank costs $\\$350$, we have\n$120x + 350y \\leq 7,000$.\n- The total number of items must be at least $25$, so we have\n$x + y \\geq 25$.\n- The farmer must buy at least $10$ bags of grain. Then, we can write\n$x \\geq 10$.\n- The farmer must also buy at least $5$ water tanks, so we have\n$y \\geq 5$.\n\nThus, putting it all together, the system of inequalities that represents this situation is\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}120 x + 350 y \\le  7 , 000 \\\\ x + y \\ge  25 \\\\ x \\ge  10 \\\\ y \\ge  5.\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186221002 1074 "First, we translate each condition into an inequality:\n\n- We're told that the total cost cannot exceed $\\[math]7,000$. Since each bag of grain costs $\\[/math]120$ and each water tank costs $\\[math]350$, we have\n$120x + 350y \\leq 7,000$.\n- The total number of items must be at least $25$, so we have\n$x + y \\geq 25$.\n- The farmer must buy at least $10$ bags of grain. Then, we can write\n$x \\geq 10$.\n- The farmer must also buy at least $5$ water tanks, so we have\n$y \\geq 5$.\n\nThus, putting it all together, the system of inequalities that represents this situation is\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}120 x + 350 y \\le  7 , 000 \\\\ x + y \\ge  25 \\\\ x \\ge  10 \\\\ y \\ge  5.\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186221003 1071 "A science teacher is buying $x$ microscopes and $y$ lab kits for student experiments. The teacher needs to purchase at least $16$ items in total, but wants to spend no more than $\\$600$. If each microscope costs $\\$50$ and each lab kit costs $\\$25$, what is the system of inequalities that best models this situation?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186221003 1071 "A science teacher is buying $x$ microscopes and $y$ lab kits for student experiments. The teacher needs to purchase at least $16$ items in total, but wants to spend no more than $\\[math]600$. If each microscope costs $\\[/math]50$ and each lab kit costs $\\[math]25$, what is the system of inequalities that best models this situation?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186221003 1074 "First, we translate each part of the problem into an inequality:\n\n- There must be at least $16$ in total, so we have\n$x + y \\geq 16$.\n- We're told that the total cost cannot exceed $\\$600$. Since each microscope costs $\\$50$ and each lab kit costs $\\$25$, we have\n$50x + 25y \\leq 600$.\n\nTherefore, putting it all together, the system of inequalities that models this situation is\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x + y \\ge  16 \\\\ 50 x + 25 y \\le  600.\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186221003 1074 "First, we translate each part of the problem into an inequality:\n\n- There must be at least $16$ in total, so we have\n$x + y \\geq 16$.\n- We're told that the total cost cannot exceed $\\[math]600$. Since each microscope costs $\\[/math]50$ and each lab kit costs $\\[math]25$, we have\n$50x + 25y \\leq 600$.\n\nTherefore, putting it all together, the system of inequalities that models this situation is\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x + y \\ge  16 \\\\ 50 x + 25 y \\le  600.\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186221005 1074 "We are told that the volunteer delivers between $5$ and $8$ gallons per trip.\n\nTo find the total number of gallons delivered in $15$ trips, we multiply both the minimum $(5)$ and maximum $(8)$ gallons by $15$. This gives\n$5 \\times 15 $\nand\n$8 \\times 15$.\n\nSo, the total number of gallons $g$ must satisfy\n\n$$\n5 \\cdot 15 \\leq g \\leq 8 \\cdot 15\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inequality is\n\n$$\n5 \\cdot 15 \\leq g \\leq 8 \\cdot 15\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186221005 1074 "We are told that the volunteer delivers between $5$ and $8$ gallons per trip.\n\nTo find the total number of gallons delivered in $15$ trips, we multiply both the minimum $(5)$ and maximum $(8)$ gallons by $15$. This gives\n$5 \\times 15 \\[5pt]$\nand\n$8 \\times 15$.\n\nSo, the total number of gallons $g$ must satisfy\n\n$$\n5 \\cdot 15 \\leq g \\leq 8 \\cdot 15\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inequality is\n\n$$\n5 \\cdot 15 \\leq g \\leq 8 \\cdot 15\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186221013 1071 "A market sold $25$ baskets of vegetables containing either carrots or onions. Each basket of carrots costs $\\$3$, and each basket of onions costs $\\$4$. If the total revenue from all sales was $\\$82$, how much, in dollars, was collected from selling the onion baskets?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186221013 1071 "A market sold $25$ baskets of vegetables containing either carrots or onions. Each basket of carrots costs $\\[math]3$, and each basket of onions costs $\\[/math]4$. If the total revenue from all sales was $\\[math]82$, how much, in dollars, was collected from selling the onion baskets?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186221013 1074 "Let $c$ and $n$ be the number of carrot and onion baskets sold, respectively.\n\nLet's establish the linear equations that model the given situation.\n\n- The market sold $25$ baskets in total. Therefore,\n$c + n = 25$.\n- The total money collected was $\\$82$. Since a carrot basket costs $\\$3$ and an onion basket costs $\\$4$, we have\n$3c + 4n = 82$.\n\nPutting these equations together, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}c + n = 25 \\\\ 3 c + 4 n = 82\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using the substitution method. We solve for $c$ in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nc + n &= 25 \\\\\nc &= 25 - n\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can substitute $c = 25 - n$ into the second equation and solve for $n{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3c + 4n &= 82 \\\\\n3(25 - n) + 4n &= 82 \\\\\n75 - 3n + 4n &= 82 \\\\\nn + 75 &= 82 \\\\\nn &= 82 - 75 \\\\\nn &= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since each onion basket costs $\\$4$, the total amount collected from the sale of onion baskets is\n\n$$\n\\$4 \\times 7 = 28\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186221013 1074 "Let $c$ and $n$ be the number of carrot and onion baskets sold, respectively.\n\nLet's establish the linear equations that model the given situation.\n\n- The market sold $25$ baskets in total. Therefore,\n$c + n = 25$.\n- The total money collected was $\\[math]82$. Since a carrot basket costs $\\[/math]3$ and an onion basket costs $\\[math]4$, we have\n$3c + 4n = 82$.\n\nPutting these equations together, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}c + n = 25 \\\\ 3 c + 4 n = 82\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using the substitution method. We solve for $c$ in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nc + n &= 25 \\\\\nc &= 25 - n\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can substitute $c = 25 - n$ into the second equation and solve for $n{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3c + 4n &= 82 \\\\\n3(25 - n) + 4n &= 82 \\\\\n75 - 3n + 4n &= 82 \\\\\nn + 75 &= 82 \\\\\nn &= 82 - 75 \\\\\nn &= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since each onion basket costs $\\[/math]4$, the total amount collected from the sale of onion baskets is\n\n$$\n\\[math]4 \\times 7 = 28\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186221014 1074 "Let $x$ and $y$ be the volume (in milliliters) of the first and second solutions, respectively.\n\nLet's establish the linear equations that model the given situation.\n\n- The total volume was $90.0\\,\\text{mL}$, so\n$x + y = 90.0$.\n- We are given that the total salt content is $25.0\\%$ of $90.0\\,\\text{mL}$. Therefore,\n$$\n\\displaystyle 25.0% \\cdot 90.0mL|= 0.25 \\cdot 90.0mL; = 22.50mL.\n$$\nWe are also told that the first solution was $10.0\\%$ salt and the second was $40.0\\%$ salt. Therefore, we get the equation\n$0.10x + 0.40y = 22.50$.\n\nPutting the equations together, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x + y = 90.0 \\\\ 0.10 x + 0.40 y = 22.50\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using the substitution method. We solve for $y$ in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + y &= 90.0 \\\\\ny &= 90.0 - x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can substitute\n\n$$\ny = 90.0 - x\n$$\n\ninto the second equation and solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.10x + 0.40y &= 22.50 \\\\\n0.10x + 0.40(90.0 - x) &= 22.50 \\\\\n0.10x + 36.0 - 0.40x &= 22.50 \\\\\n- 0.30x &= -13.50 \\\\\nx &= \\frac{-13.50}{-0.30} \\\\\nx &= 45.0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the first solution was $10.0\\%$ salt by volume, the volume of salt in the first solution used was\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.10x &= 0.10(45.0) \\\\\n&= 4.5mL\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186221014 1074 "Let $x$ and $y$ be the volume (in milliliters) of the first and second solutions, respectively.\n\nLet's establish the linear equations that model the given situation.\n\n- The total volume was $90.0\\,\\text{mL}$, so\n$x + y = 90.0$.\n- We are given that the total salt content is $25.0\\%$ of $90.0\\,\\text{mL}$. Therefore,\n$$\n\\displaystyle 25.0\\% \\cdot 90.0mL|= 0.25 \\cdot 90.0mL; = 22.50mL.\n$$\nWe are also told that the first solution was $10.0\\%$ salt and the second was $40.0\\%$ salt. Therefore, we get the equation\n$0.10x + 0.40y = 22.50$.\n\nPutting the equations together, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x + y = 90.0 \\\\ 0.10 x + 0.40 y = 22.50\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using the substitution method. We solve for $y$ in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + y &= 90.0 \\\\\ny &= 90.0 - x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can substitute\n\n$$\ny = 90.0 - x\n$$\n\ninto the second equation and solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.10x + 0.40y &= 22.50 \\\\\n0.10x + 0.40(90.0 - x) &= 22.50 \\\\\n0.10x + 36.0 - 0.40x &= 22.50 \\\\\n- 0.30x &= -13.50 \\\\\nx &= \\frac{-13.50}{-0.30} \\\\\nx &= 45.0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the first solution was $10.0\\%$ salt by volume, the volume of salt in the first solution used was\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.10x &= 0.10(45.0) \\\\\n&= 4.5mL\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186221016 1071 "At a restaurant, one diner paid $\\$155.50$ for $6$ main courses and $5$ drinks. Another diner paid $\\$72.50$ for $3$ main courses and $2$ drinks.\n\nWhat is the cost, in dollars, of one drink?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186221016 1071 "At a restaurant, one diner paid $\\[math]155.50$ for $6$ main courses and $5$ drinks. Another diner paid $\\[/math]72.50$ for $3$ main courses and $2$ drinks.\n\nWhat is the cost, in dollars, of one drink?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186221016 1074 "Let $m$ and $d$ be the cost of one main course and the cost of one drink, respectively.\n\nLet's establish the linear equations that model the given situation.\n\n- The first diner paid $\\$155.50$ for $6$ mains and $5$ drinks, so we have\n$6m + 5d = 155.50$.\n- The second diner paid $\\$72.50$ for $3$ mains and $2$ drinks, so we have\n$3m + 2d = 72.50$.\n\nPutting these equations together, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}6 m + 5 d = 155.50 \\\\ 3 m + 2 d = 72.50\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nWe will solve the above system using the elimination method. Notice that the $m$-coefficient in the first equation is ${\\color{red}{6}}$, while in the second equation it is ${\\color{blue}{3}}$.\n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $-{\\color{red}{2}}$, then the $m$-coefficient in the first and second equations will be $6$ and $-6$, respectively, and they will cancel when we add the equations.\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}\\hspace{0.167em} \\hspace{0.167em} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\color{red}6} m + 5 d = 155.50 \\\\ ( \\hspace{0.167em} {\\color{blue}3} m + 2 d = 72.50 \\hspace{0.167em} ) \\hspace{0.222em} \\times  ( - {\\color{red}2} )\\end{array} \\right. }\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}6 m + 5 d = 155.50 \\\\ - 6 m - 4 d = - 145.00\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $m$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle \\cancel{6 m} + 5 d & \\displaystyle  = 155.50 \\\\ \\displaystyle - \\cancel{6 m} - 4 d & \\displaystyle  = - 145.00 \\\\ \\hline \\displaystyle ( 5 d - 4 d ) & \\displaystyle  = ( 155.50 - 145.00 ) \\\\ \\displaystyle d & \\displaystyle  = 10.50\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the cost of one drink is $\\$10.50$." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186221016 1074 "Let $m$ and $d$ be the cost of one main course and the cost of one drink, respectively.\n\nLet's establish the linear equations that model the given situation.\n\n- The first diner paid $\\[math]155.50$ for $6$ mains and $5$ drinks, so we have\n$6m + 5d = 155.50$.\n- The second diner paid $\\[/math]72.50$ for $3$ mains and $2$ drinks, so we have\n$3m + 2d = 72.50$.\n\nPutting these equations together, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}6 m + 5 d = 155.50 \\\\ 3 m + 2 d = 72.50\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nWe will solve the above system using the elimination method. Notice that the $m$-coefficient in the first equation is ${\\color{red}{6}}$, while in the second equation it is ${\\color{blue}{3}}$.\n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $-{\\color{red}{2}}$, then the $m$-coefficient in the first and second equations will be $6$ and $-6$, respectively, and they will cancel when we add the equations.\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}\\hspace{0.167em} \\hspace{0.167em} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\color{red}6} m + 5 d = 155.50 \\\\ ( \\hspace{0.167em} {\\color{blue}3} m + 2 d = 72.50 \\hspace{0.167em} ) \\hspace{0.222em} \\times  ( - {\\color{red}2} )\\end{array} \\right. }\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}6 m + 5 d = 155.50 \\\\ - 6 m - 4 d = - 145.00\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $m$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle \\cancel{6 m} + 5 d & \\displaystyle  = 155.50 \\\\ \\displaystyle - \\cancel{6 m} - 4 d & \\displaystyle  = - 145.00 \\\\ \\hline \\displaystyle ( 5 d - 4 d ) & \\displaystyle  = ( 155.50 - 145.00 ) \\\\ \\displaystyle d & \\displaystyle  = 10.50\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the cost of one drink is $\\[math]10.50$." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186221041 1071 "![](images/79/79d056ab1a6587e2ce9ab61e7567832d1bbd1608b6529da875e98c2848e1cb3a.png)\n\nTo fund a class trip, Leo sells boxes of pencils and pens. He earns $\\$7$ for each box of pencils sold and $\\$14$ for each box of pens sold. The graph above shows all possible combinations of boxes Leo could sell to earn $t$ dollars. What is the value of $t$?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186221041 1071 "![](images/79/79d056ab1a6587e2ce9ab61e7567832d1bbd1608b6529da875e98c2848e1cb3a.png)\n\nTo fund a class trip, Leo sells boxes of pencils and pens. He earns $\\[math]7$ for each box of pencils sold and $\\[/math]14$ for each box of pens sold. The graph above shows all possible combinations of boxes Leo could sell to earn $t$ dollars. What is the value of $t$?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186221041 1074 "To find Leo’s total earnings $t$, we use the earnings per box and apply the coordinates from the graph to evaluate $t$ using the linear equation that models the situation.\n\nLet $x$ be the number of boxes of pencils and $y$ be the number of boxes of pens.\n\nThus, the equation that translates this situation is\n\n$$\n7x + 14y = t\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we see:\n\n- The line crosses the $y$-axis at $(0,10)$, this means if he sold $10$ boxes of pens and $0$ of pencils, he earned\n$$\n\\displaystyle t|= 7x + 14y; = 7(0) + 14(10); = 140.\n$$\n- The line crosses the $x$-axis at $(20,0)$, this means if he sold $20$ boxes of pencils and $0$ of pens, he earned\n$$\n\\displaystyle t|= 7x + 14y; = 7(20) + 14(0); = 140.\n$$\n\nTherefore, in either case, Leo would have earned a total of\n\n$$\nt = \\$140\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186221041 1074 "To find Leo’s total earnings $t$, we use the earnings per box and apply the coordinates from the graph to evaluate $t$ using the linear equation that models the situation.\n\nLet $x$ be the number of boxes of pencils and $y$ be the number of boxes of pens.\n\nThus, the equation that translates this situation is\n\n$$\n7x + 14y = t\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we see:\n\n- The line crosses the $y$-axis at $(0,10)$, this means if he sold $10$ boxes of pens and $0$ of pencils, he earned\n$$\n\\displaystyle t|= 7x + 14y; = 7(0) + 14(10); = 140.\n$$\n- The line crosses the $x$-axis at $(20,0)$, this means if he sold $20$ boxes of pencils and $0$ of pens, he earned\n$$\n\\displaystyle t|= 7x + 14y; = 7(20) + 14(0); = 140.\n$$\n\nTherefore, in either case, Leo would have earned a total of\n\n$$\nt = \\[math]140\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186221060 1071 "![](images/f6/f60c95a8e0bb48a093fafa3754351a715572fcea7c1dd4740c61ecc0ca8f4621.png)\n\nThe graph shows the possible combinations of the number of notebooks and pens that could be purchased for $\\$21$ at a school supply store. If Mia purchased pens and $6$ notebooks for a total of $\\$21$, how many pens did she buy?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186221060 1071 "![](images/f6/f60c95a8e0bb48a093fa